00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:42.830
For example, you
might be tempted

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:44.870
to use a single
project within which

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:46.820
to store all of
your applications

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:48.200
or run all of your applications.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:51.110
That may work for you, if
you're a small mom and pop shop,

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:52.530
or even an enterprise customer.

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:56.000
But we found that it doesn't
work for us because we can't

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.090
easily split resources
to align with how

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:01.790
we want to manage that cost.

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:04.850
How you organize your resources,
your org, your folders,

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:06.530
your projects,
your applications,

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:10.100
the products inside
of those projects,

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:14.930
has an enormous impact on
how you look at your bill.

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:18.660
Number two-- organize
all of these resources

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:21.000
to leverage fingerprint
where at all possible.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.870
Those fingerprints should align
to how you want to manage cost.

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:27.810
Fingerprints are going to
survive the test of time,

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:31.830
as your organization changes,
as ownership of an application

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:36.420
changes, as code names
for your projects change.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:40.710
And three-- please, don't
