00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:10.388
We'll find out.

00:04:10.388 --> 00:04:12.680
And that's going to require
just a little bit of setup.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:21.279
Target has, as I just
mentioned, almost 2,000 stores,

00:04:21.279 --> 00:04:24.250
distribution centers,
warehouses, data centers,

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:27.070
et cetera, nationwide.

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:29.920
And there are select
aspects at every single one

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:33.760
of those locations,
such that that location

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:36.550
could be considered a,
quote unquote, cloud

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:40.510
environment across the board.

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:41.920
We have infrastructure there.

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:43.240
We have platforms there.

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:44.650
We have applications there.

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:47.140
And due to the
geographic distribution

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:50.200
and the sometimes
limited connectivity,

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:52.330
based on where
those locations are,

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:55.030
each store, each
site I should say,

00:04:55.030 --> 00:05:00.580
is treated as a standalone,
isolated cloud environment.

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:03.790
You could call them, for lack
of a better word, mini-clouds,

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:05.620
right?

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:08.440
We also have our own data
centers with similar resources,

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:10.280
there's just a lot more of them.

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:12.850
So maybe those are
not so mini-clouds,

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:14.560
not quite mega clouds.

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:16.480
And then since we're
giving this talk,

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:18.850
it should be pretty obvious
and should surprise no one

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:22.720
that we also use public cloud
providers, such as Google's

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:24.273
cloud.

00:05:24.273 --> 00:05:25.940
All right, we'll call
them a mega cloud.

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:27.730
That's fine.

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:30.850
To us-- and this is the most
important part that I can get

00:05:30.850 --> 00:05:32.770
across in this slide--

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:39.150
we want to treat all of these
resources the exact same way, e

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:41.790
pluribus unum, if I can, right?

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:44.520
Regardless of the disparity
in the infrastructure,

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:49.260
we want apps runnable everywhere
in the same exact way,

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:52.440
the Target way, not
the store's way,

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:56.190
not the distribution center
way, not Google's way,

00:05:56.190 --> 00:05:57.720
but the Target way.

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:00.630
The infrastructure is all
managed slightly differently.

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:03.600
And that means that we
need some sort of platform

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:06.300
in the middle there
between the infrastructure

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:11.990
and the applications in
order to be truly ubiquitous.

00:06:11.990 --> 00:06:13.900
So we wrote a thing.

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:18.460
And we call it the Target
Application Platform, or TAP.

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:19.630
The idea is simple.

00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:23.680
Given every possible compute
location that Target uses,

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:26.440
the experience of
deploying an application

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:30.685
to any of those locations
should be exactly the same.

00:06:30.685 --> 00:06:32.560
Now, if you were at the
keynote this morning,

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:36.690
you heard something from
Google similar, called Anthos.

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:37.940
I think I'm saying that right.

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:40.930
I've frankly only heard it
once or twice this morning.

00:06:40.930 --> 00:06:43.870
And TAP, I want you to
know, we want you to know,

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:46.147
just hit its one year
anniversary four days ago

00:06:46.147 --> 00:06:48.230
and pretty much kind of
does the same exact thing.

00:06:48.230 --> 00:06:50.600
So yay, Target.

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:52.390
OK.

00:06:52.390 --> 00:06:55.660
The intricacies of TAP's
design, how we got there,

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:58.120
why we did it, those
are all the contents

00:06:58.120 --> 00:06:59.450
of another presentation.

00:06:59.450 --> 00:07:02.650
And that YouTube link, should
you want to take a picture

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:04.360
or take a look at
the slide deck later,

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:07.480
will take you to last
year's presentation

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:11.740
on the design of TAP that
we gave at Google Next '18,

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:13.780
in July.

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:16.060
Or if you have
time this evening,

00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:19.780
you can attend Dev 217, given
by some of my colleagues

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:21.950
tonight at 5:00 PM.

00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:22.450
All right.

00:07:22.450 --> 00:07:25.390
The bottom line, regardless, is
that we have clouds everywhere.

00:07:25.390 --> 00:07:28.900
And we try to manage and want
to manage what runs there

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:30.920
all in the same exact way.

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:35.722
So what does that mean for
how Target uses Google Cloud?

00:07:35.722 --> 00:07:40.470
All right, we have
two primary goals

00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:43.920
in leveraging any
cloud provider.

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:49.370
The first-- we want to put the
things that impact our guests--

00:07:49.370 --> 00:07:51.730
our customers, that's
our term for guests,

00:07:51.730 --> 00:07:54.250
if you're not familiar
with this parlance--

00:07:54.250 --> 00:07:56.410
as close to the
guest as possible.

00:07:56.410 --> 00:07:59.440
And regardless of the
kinds of workloads

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:00.947
that we could run
there, the things

00:08:00.947 --> 00:08:02.530
that make the most
sense at any public

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:07.030
cloud provider, our target.com,
our mobile apps, the things

00:08:07.030 --> 00:08:09.610
that you, when you
walk into a store,

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:12.390
experience right then and there.

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:13.890
And the second thing
is that we want

00:08:13.890 --> 00:08:16.560
to do it in the most
cost-effective way possible.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:19.390
And that kind of becomes
a tricky situation

00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:21.390
and the point of the talk.

00:08:21.390 --> 00:08:22.120
Scale?

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:24.007
Eh, public cloud
providers all scale.

00:08:24.007 --> 00:08:24.840
That comes for free.

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:27.680
Google does it better
than everybody else.

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:30.210
I'm biased, anyway.

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:35.760
But even if we weren't using
TAP or any other sort of Target

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:38.700
custom built tool to
deploy our workloads out

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:43.120
to Google, putting stuff
out at a cloud provider,

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:44.700
it's dead easy, right?

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:46.380
But making sure
that we're using it

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:48.210
in the most effective
way possible,

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:50.130
so much harder than we thought.

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:51.630
So much harder than we thought.

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:53.755
You probably do-- in fact,
I'm just going to pause

00:08:53.755 --> 00:08:54.630
from the script here.

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:57.780
Who thinks that
it's hard to track

00:08:57.780 --> 00:08:59.940
how you're using
your applications out

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:01.800
of Google by a show of hands?

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:02.300
Right?

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:02.800
OK.

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:03.840
Well, [MUMBLES].

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:05.820
Take 20%, nah, 10%.

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:07.240
That's all right.

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:09.674
Boy, that makes me want to ask
why everybody else is here.

00:09:09.674 --> 00:09:11.490
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:11.490 --> 00:09:13.920
Even if we weren't doing
that, it's super hard.

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:16.530
So why?

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:18.270
Why is it hard?

00:09:18.270 --> 00:09:20.460
Some of that is based on
the choices we've made.

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:22.860
And some of that
is frankly based

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:25.660
on the structure of
how Google does things,

00:09:25.660 --> 00:09:27.150
of how we've chosen
to use Google,

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:30.300
and of how Google allows
themselves to be used.

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:31.230
We'll talk about both.

00:09:34.335 --> 00:09:36.710
RACHEL SHINN: So billing is
a hot topic at Google, right?

00:09:36.710 --> 00:09:38.293
We're talking about
money here, and it

00:09:38.293 --> 00:09:39.800
gets a lot of visibility.

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:41.810
So gets a lot of questions
with it, questions

00:09:41.810 --> 00:09:43.970
from senior leadership,
to our finance

00:09:43.970 --> 00:09:46.070
teams, all of the
infrastructure teams

00:09:46.070 --> 00:09:48.320
that deploy managed services
on Google Cloud, and then

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:50.028
all the way down to
the application teams

00:09:50.028 --> 00:09:52.120
that deploy their apps
on Google Cloud as well.

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:53.480
So you can kind of
break these questions

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:54.830
into two different categories.

00:09:54.830 --> 00:09:57.890
Reactive-- so I get the bill,
and I'm like, holy crap,

00:09:57.890 --> 00:09:59.630
why is this so big?

00:09:59.630 --> 00:10:01.220
What am I spending my money on?

00:10:01.220 --> 00:10:03.080
Where are these
services coming from?

00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:06.680
Storage, compute, databases,
logging, or something else.

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:08.200
And then there's proactive.

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:09.950
So how do I know if
this is a good number?

00:10:09.950 --> 00:10:11.570
Is it too high,
or is it too low?

00:10:11.570 --> 00:10:13.460
Or is this just bad?

00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:16.010
How do I be more effective in
what I deploy on Google Cloud?

00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:18.090
How do I understand
how to be better?

00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:20.833
Or how do I understand
how to lower this number?

00:10:20.833 --> 00:10:22.250
And so there's one
question that I

00:10:22.250 --> 00:10:23.667
want you to think
about every time

00:10:23.667 --> 00:10:25.750
we talk about something
up here, every time you're

00:10:25.750 --> 00:10:28.055
going to make a decision
on a tool or a service,

00:10:28.055 --> 00:10:31.400
and that is really, how do I
provide the most information

00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:34.746
that teams can use in order
to make better decisions?

00:10:34.746 --> 00:10:35.980
And the next slide here.

00:10:35.980 --> 00:10:36.523
Yep.

00:10:36.523 --> 00:10:38.690
So we're going to talk a
lot about things that we've

00:10:38.690 --> 00:10:40.580
done in the past today, things
that we're currently doing,

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:42.020
and then things that we
want to do in the future.

00:10:42.020 --> 00:10:43.280
And I'm going to
pause a few times,

00:10:43.280 --> 00:10:44.910
and I'm going to
ask this question.

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:46.160
And based off of
the answer, that's

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:48.327
how we're going to decide
on what we're going to do.

00:10:50.530 --> 00:10:51.670
RON CUIRLE: All right.

00:10:51.670 --> 00:10:54.730
So there's a long journey to
put stuff out of the cloud.

00:10:54.730 --> 00:10:56.850
The larger the enterprise,
likely sometimes

00:10:56.850 --> 00:10:58.660
longer the journey is.

00:10:58.660 --> 00:11:01.300
But whenever you
start on any journey,

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:03.730
experience, your own
personal experience,

00:11:03.730 --> 00:11:06.190
is going to tell you
that there are things

00:11:06.190 --> 00:11:08.200
that you can be pretty
sure you're going to run

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:10.390
into along the way, right?

00:11:10.390 --> 00:11:13.780
Some of those things you'll know
exactly what to do about it.

00:11:13.780 --> 00:11:15.910
Before you even
get there, you know

00:11:15.910 --> 00:11:17.860
you'll just have to
deal with the front desk

00:11:17.860 --> 00:11:19.210
clerk at the hotel.

00:11:19.210 --> 00:11:20.920
And you can't change
their attitude.

00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:25.023
And life is just going to suck
for those first few seconds.

00:11:25.023 --> 00:11:27.190
So you're either going to
just have to deal with it,

00:11:27.190 --> 00:11:31.330
climb over that hurdle,
or go around it somehow.

00:11:31.330 --> 00:11:34.330
We can't control the
weather, but we can certainly

00:11:34.330 --> 00:11:36.670
take a look at the forecast
and dress appropriately,

00:11:36.670 --> 00:11:38.890
or bring an umbrella.

00:11:38.890 --> 00:11:42.100
But there are also things on
any journey that trip you up

00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:44.770
along the way and knock
you flat on your face

00:11:44.770 --> 00:11:47.920
when you're not
expecting them to happen.

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:50.770
When Target first started
moving workloads into Google,

00:11:50.770 --> 00:11:54.340
we did not do it in a
big bang kind of way.

00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:56.200
It was piecemeal, a
little bit at a time.

00:11:56.200 --> 00:11:59.320
We were inching our way
in while migrating out

00:11:59.320 --> 00:12:01.980
of another cloud provider.

00:12:01.980 --> 00:12:04.630
We knew some of what
we were walking into,

00:12:04.630 --> 00:12:09.480
and we very much unknowingly
brought a whole ton of baggage

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:11.250
with us.

00:12:11.250 --> 00:12:16.140
Billing was absolutely
not a primary thought,

00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:19.150
but it was there, right?

00:12:19.150 --> 00:12:19.870
We want to know.

00:12:19.870 --> 00:12:21.350
We want to be able
to control it.

00:12:21.350 --> 00:12:22.780
But it wasn't a primitive.

00:12:22.780 --> 00:12:25.420
It wasn't considered
something that was key

00:12:25.420 --> 00:12:27.590
when we started this journey.

00:12:27.590 --> 00:12:30.850
So this piecemeal
method, unfortunately,

00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:33.220
led us to making some
decisions and choices that

00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:35.440
made sense at the
time, that later turned

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:37.780
into the kinds of things that
you trip over because you

00:12:37.780 --> 00:12:39.238
didn't see it in
the path and makes

00:12:39.238 --> 00:12:41.800
you fall flat on your face.

00:12:41.800 --> 00:12:45.640
And some of those things made
sense at the previous provider,

00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:47.830
like a singular project.

00:12:47.830 --> 00:12:51.970
It made sense there, doesn't
make sense here to us.

00:12:51.970 --> 00:12:54.010
At Google, all of
a sudden, we were

00:12:54.010 --> 00:12:59.080
thrust into a situation
where we had to give better

00:12:59.080 --> 00:13:01.030
information to our developers.

00:13:01.030 --> 00:13:03.767
And we weren't well
equipped to do it.

00:13:03.767 --> 00:13:05.350
Everything's a
journey, though, right?

00:13:05.350 --> 00:13:06.430
You've got missteps.

00:13:06.430 --> 00:13:07.540
You've got backtracks.

00:13:07.540 --> 00:13:08.380
You've got bridge building.

00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:09.380
It's par for the course.

00:13:09.380 --> 00:13:11.050
So let's talk about
some of the things

00:13:11.050 --> 00:13:15.890
we saw before we ever
started this migration.

00:13:15.890 --> 00:13:17.220
Let's call them big rocks.

00:13:17.220 --> 00:13:20.340
These are the things you know
you're going to run into.

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:23.270
At Target, we're
absolutely not going

00:13:23.270 --> 00:13:25.640
to be able to change the
organizational structure

00:13:25.640 --> 00:13:29.060
of our company from
the ground up to suit

00:13:29.060 --> 00:13:32.460
what is possible at a
given cloud provider.

00:13:32.460 --> 00:13:34.880
So we need a way to
identify a resource

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:38.390
and have that reportable
at every level of the org.

00:13:38.390 --> 00:13:40.970
That rock, we kind of
chose to ignore going in,

00:13:40.970 --> 00:13:42.463
even though we
knew it was there.

00:13:42.463 --> 00:13:43.880
We didn't treat
it as a primitive.

00:13:43.880 --> 00:13:49.700
Don't make that same choice
that we made, please.

00:13:49.700 --> 00:13:52.730
Secondarily, while
most companies actually

00:13:52.730 --> 00:13:56.120
split up their
bill and pass that

00:13:56.120 --> 00:13:58.800
along to various parts
of their organization

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:03.830
either for direct payment or
internal transfer of funds,

00:14:03.830 --> 00:14:07.430
Target takes that
bill, splits it up,

00:14:07.430 --> 00:14:10.920
and shows every level
of our organization

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:12.380
what they've spent.

00:14:12.380 --> 00:14:17.300
But we don't expect any
payment in return for that.

00:14:17.300 --> 00:14:20.420
One part of the org is
responsible for paying

00:14:20.420 --> 00:14:25.850
all infrastructure costs
associated with that provider.

00:14:25.850 --> 00:14:28.760
And that part alone is
responsible for the cost

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:31.160
control associated
with that bill.

00:14:31.160 --> 00:14:32.820
So we all share responsibility.

00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:34.880
In summary here, we all
share responsibility

00:14:34.880 --> 00:14:38.630
in contributing to that
bill, but only one party

00:14:38.630 --> 00:14:42.780
is accountable to
its management.

00:14:42.780 --> 00:14:46.210
That can be a problem.

00:14:46.210 --> 00:14:48.660
Imagine you're an
application team.

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:51.360
And you're sitting
there, and people

00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:52.890
are telling you, spend less.

00:14:52.890 --> 00:14:55.200
Why do you care?

00:14:55.200 --> 00:14:58.920
You are neither accountable, it
doesn't impact you financially,

00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:01.260
it potentially impacts
you technologically

00:15:01.260 --> 00:15:04.260
to change the way that you're
running your application.

00:15:04.260 --> 00:15:05.020
Why do you care?

00:15:05.020 --> 00:15:07.420
We'll talk more about
that a little bit later.

00:15:07.420 --> 00:15:10.800
But just keep in mind
that we have no carrot

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:14.790
or stick to change a team's
decisions about how they

00:15:14.790 --> 00:15:17.790
use their resources at Google.

00:15:17.790 --> 00:15:21.600
And lastly, that
last bullet point--

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:23.430
bring your own Kubernetes.

00:15:23.430 --> 00:15:25.170
We knew from the
very, very beginning

00:15:25.170 --> 00:15:29.040
that we would not be using GKE,
but bringing our own Kubernetes

00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:31.950
clusters to Google Cloud.

00:15:31.950 --> 00:15:34.920
If you're familiar with
Kubernetes, either older

00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:37.650
versions of GKE or some of
the more recent versions

00:15:37.650 --> 00:15:41.670
of Kubernetes Native,
you know that the idea

00:15:41.670 --> 00:15:45.810
of shared resources is great
from a compute efficiency

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:46.570
point of view.

00:15:46.570 --> 00:15:51.240
But tracking who used what
when can be kind of hard.

00:15:51.240 --> 00:15:53.730
So we knew upfront we
were going to need a way

00:15:53.730 --> 00:15:57.480
to apportion our
Kubernetes VM bill, such

00:15:57.480 --> 00:16:00.600
that we knew how a
namespace was being used

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:02.910
in a relatively accurate way.

00:16:02.910 --> 00:16:03.410
All right.

00:16:03.410 --> 00:16:04.350
So these are the
things that weren't

00:16:04.350 --> 00:16:06.615
going to change when we moved
our workloads to Google.

00:16:06.615 --> 00:16:09.240
We still have to know how we're
going to climb over these rocks

00:16:09.240 --> 00:16:12.360
and achieve what
we want to achieve.

00:16:12.360 --> 00:16:15.450
It turns out that every
big cloud provider also

00:16:15.450 --> 00:16:19.990
has their own big rocks that
you absolutely can't change.

00:16:19.990 --> 00:16:24.540
At Google, one of
those is labels.

00:16:24.540 --> 00:16:28.680
Remember, we want the holy grail
of enabling these teams to make

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:31.990
their own better decisions.

00:16:31.990 --> 00:16:34.830
Labeling, very
popular way, not new,

00:16:34.830 --> 00:16:37.980
to add more meta information
to your resources.

00:16:37.980 --> 00:16:40.000
And they even end up
in billing exports

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:42.690
which, if you spend a lot of
time in them, is so fantastic.

00:16:42.690 --> 00:16:44.250
You've got attributions
to send back.

00:16:44.250 --> 00:16:46.380
Life is great.

00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:50.220
But there are products,
like Stackdriver Logging,

00:16:50.220 --> 00:16:52.530
that records
information coming in

00:16:52.530 --> 00:16:59.460
from resources, like HTTP load
balancers, that can be labeled.

00:16:59.460 --> 00:17:03.270
But the resulting logs from
those resources cannot be

00:17:03.270 --> 00:17:06.630
apportioned, even though they
have their own cost associated

00:17:06.630 --> 00:17:08.710
with them.

00:17:08.710 --> 00:17:14.700
For example, in this screenshot,
cloud HTTP load balancers,

00:17:14.700 --> 00:17:19.859
that particular resource, the
log that is load balancer logs,

00:17:19.859 --> 00:17:21.569
cannot be labeled.

00:17:21.569 --> 00:17:24.270
But remember, we also
have one project up front.

00:17:24.270 --> 00:17:28.710
All of our applications are
sharing this log source.

00:17:28.710 --> 00:17:34.440
How do I find out that
application A is largely

00:17:34.440 --> 00:17:38.160
responsible for 50 or more
percent of all of the costs

00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:42.840
going in there and give that
team accurate show back?

00:17:42.840 --> 00:17:44.820
Help them manage
themselves, right?

00:17:44.820 --> 00:17:46.750
Remember, we started
with that single project,

00:17:46.750 --> 00:17:48.750
and we just kept moving
new applications in.

00:17:48.750 --> 00:17:50.760
So the more apps we had,
the harder it became.

00:17:53.320 --> 00:17:56.420
And now, let's talk about
some of the tripping stones.

00:17:56.420 --> 00:17:56.920
All right.

00:17:56.920 --> 00:18:00.430
I think that they came in three
different kinds of categories.

00:18:00.430 --> 00:18:03.600
First, there are
things that we knew

00:18:03.600 --> 00:18:08.340
we'd be creating tech debt for,
if we did it a certain way.

00:18:08.340 --> 00:18:10.290
But we did it anyway,
thinking to ourselves,

00:18:10.290 --> 00:18:11.908
we'll deal with it later.

00:18:11.908 --> 00:18:13.200
OK, now I want a show of hands.

00:18:13.200 --> 00:18:14.620
Who does that all the time?

00:18:14.620 --> 00:18:15.120
Thank you.

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:15.510
RACHEL SHINN: Everybody.

00:18:15.510 --> 00:18:15.840
RON CUIRLE: Thank you.

00:18:15.840 --> 00:18:17.880
You are lying if you
do not raise your hand.

00:18:17.880 --> 00:18:19.440
That's fine.

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:22.650
The second category is
that we couldn't do it

00:18:22.650 --> 00:18:25.980
the way that we assumed
we could because of either

00:18:25.980 --> 00:18:28.200
a misunderstanding, or
some reason or another,

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:30.150
about our interpretation
of the docs,

00:18:30.150 --> 00:18:32.770
or the way the docs were
written, or who knows what?

00:18:32.770 --> 00:18:37.830
And the third category is that
we didn't know we could do it,

00:18:37.830 --> 00:18:41.340
or we didn't know that
we had to do it in order

00:18:41.340 --> 00:18:43.980
to get that piece
of information.

00:18:43.980 --> 00:18:47.820
So let's talk about
some of those items.

00:18:47.820 --> 00:18:50.260
At our previous cloud
provider, for example,

00:18:50.260 --> 00:18:53.160
we had established a
naming convention such

00:18:53.160 --> 00:18:57.880
that an app and component
combo would uniquely

00:18:57.880 --> 00:19:02.300
identify the application
utilizing a resource.

00:19:02.300 --> 00:19:06.550
So that would even address
certain security startup

00:19:06.550 --> 00:19:10.600
problems, like let's make
sure that if you are not

00:19:10.600 --> 00:19:13.330
a registered application
or a registered component,

00:19:13.330 --> 00:19:15.880
we're not going to allow you
to read these secrets out

00:19:15.880 --> 00:19:16.692
of our platform.

00:19:16.692 --> 00:19:19.150
We're not going to allow you
to read these key value pairs.

00:19:19.150 --> 00:19:22.900
We're not going to allow you
to register your services.

00:19:22.900 --> 00:19:24.730
And because-- oh.

00:19:24.730 --> 00:19:27.880
Actually, let me back up just
one moment and give an example.

00:19:27.880 --> 00:19:29.800
Think about application
and component.

00:19:29.800 --> 00:19:33.430
Think about price
as an application.

00:19:33.430 --> 00:19:35.320
And within price, we
might have an API.

00:19:35.320 --> 00:19:38.350
We might have a Redis
component of these things.

00:19:38.350 --> 00:19:42.160
They all roll up to
this price application,

00:19:42.160 --> 00:19:44.500
but everything's got
their own component.

00:19:44.500 --> 00:19:45.580
This is legacy.

00:19:45.580 --> 00:19:48.140
This is previous cloud provider.

00:19:48.140 --> 00:19:51.670
But did you know that
application teams love

00:19:51.670 --> 00:19:54.430
code names for what they write?

00:19:54.430 --> 00:19:55.840
OK, at least I see some smiles.

00:19:55.840 --> 00:19:58.540
This is good.

00:19:58.540 --> 00:20:01.180
And what happens when
those code names change?

00:20:01.180 --> 00:20:03.230
What happens when a new
leader comes in and says,

00:20:03.230 --> 00:20:04.938
I don't want to own
that product anymore.

00:20:04.938 --> 00:20:06.040
Give it to so-and-so.

00:20:06.040 --> 00:20:08.540
All of a sudden, the
application still exists.

00:20:08.540 --> 00:20:10.550
The product still exists.

00:20:10.550 --> 00:20:13.750
The components may change,
but it no longer rolls up

00:20:13.750 --> 00:20:16.370
to the same portion
of the organization.

00:20:16.370 --> 00:20:18.830
And that's not good.

00:20:18.830 --> 00:20:22.640
That's not good for
tracking cost over time.

00:20:22.640 --> 00:20:25.330
The first apps that
we put out at Google

00:20:25.330 --> 00:20:28.730
only used compute and
network resources.

00:20:28.730 --> 00:20:31.390
It's important to know,
because maybe this is partly

00:20:31.390 --> 00:20:34.940
defensive to say, we kind
of knew it might happen,

00:20:34.940 --> 00:20:36.370
but that's all right.

00:20:36.370 --> 00:20:37.300
There was no BigQuery.

00:20:37.300 --> 00:20:38.342
There was no Stackdriver.

00:20:38.342 --> 00:20:40.900
There was no anything,
except compute and network.

00:20:40.900 --> 00:20:44.140
Because remember, we
wanted an agnostic way

00:20:44.140 --> 00:20:46.310
to be able to use
those primitives

00:20:46.310 --> 00:20:50.470
that every public cloud
provider offered in order

00:20:50.470 --> 00:20:56.260
to have our platform be agnostic
and deployable to any location.

00:20:56.260 --> 00:20:58.030
Putting ourselves
into a single project

00:20:58.030 --> 00:21:00.250
totally made sense
at the time, but how

00:21:00.250 --> 00:21:03.112
do you divvy up those things?

00:21:03.112 --> 00:21:04.070
RACHEL SHINN: Alrighty.

00:21:04.070 --> 00:21:06.770
So some of the
challenges that we faced.

00:21:06.770 --> 00:21:09.350
This is just a basic
output of our billing data

00:21:09.350 --> 00:21:10.042
flow looks like.

00:21:10.042 --> 00:21:11.750
And it should look
pretty similar, right?

00:21:11.750 --> 00:21:13.550
You go into the Cloud console
under the Billing section,

00:21:13.550 --> 00:21:15.100
and you hit Export My Data.

00:21:15.100 --> 00:21:17.270
It all goes into a Google
Cloud Storage bucket.

00:21:17.270 --> 00:21:19.160
And then it gets
sent into BigQuery.

00:21:19.160 --> 00:21:21.110
From there, we created
a handful of views

00:21:21.110 --> 00:21:24.093
that we could actually visualize
this data in a different tool.

00:21:24.093 --> 00:21:25.760
And the two tools
that we primarily used

00:21:25.760 --> 00:21:27.590
were Data Studio and Grafana.

00:21:27.590 --> 00:21:30.140
And just for reference, Data
Studio is a Google product,

00:21:30.140 --> 00:21:32.190
but it is not a
Google Cloud product.

00:21:32.190 --> 00:21:34.640
However, it is very
easy to integrate

00:21:34.640 --> 00:21:36.170
the data between the two.

00:21:36.170 --> 00:21:38.510
And then Grafana is just
another visualization tool

00:21:38.510 --> 00:21:41.208
that Target uses as part of
our measurement platform.

00:21:41.208 --> 00:21:43.250
So this is what the billing
data flow looks like.

00:21:43.250 --> 00:21:45.710
Two big caveats that
I want to call out

00:21:45.710 --> 00:21:48.322
is that, first, our teams don't
have access to actually go

00:21:48.322 --> 00:21:49.280
into the Cloud console.

00:21:49.280 --> 00:21:51.197
They don't have those
[INAUDIBLE] permissions.

00:21:51.197 --> 00:21:53.938
We use other tools to actually
deploy such a Spinnaker.

00:21:53.938 --> 00:21:55.730
Or for teams that are
deploying containers,

00:21:55.730 --> 00:21:57.928
you can just directly
from the command line.

00:21:57.928 --> 00:22:00.470
So that kind of posed a problem
when we wanted to use labels,

00:22:00.470 --> 00:22:00.970
right?

00:22:00.970 --> 00:22:03.260
Because labels was an option,
but it wasn't enforced.

00:22:03.260 --> 00:22:04.460
It wasn't regulated.

00:22:04.460 --> 00:22:06.803
So therefore, teams
just didn't do it.

00:22:06.803 --> 00:22:08.720
So that was the first
challenge that we really

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:09.860
needed to figure
out is, how do we

00:22:09.860 --> 00:22:11.693
go through and apply
those labels when teams

00:22:11.693 --> 00:22:13.350
aren't doing it themselves?

00:22:13.350 --> 00:22:15.140
So our short-term
solution was to write

00:22:15.140 --> 00:22:18.020
a script that retroactively
goes in a couple of times a day

00:22:18.020 --> 00:22:20.485
and looks at all the new
instances that are created,

00:22:20.485 --> 00:22:21.860
grabs the instance
name, and then

00:22:21.860 --> 00:22:25.700
applies that name as a label
under a key value pair.

00:22:25.700 --> 00:22:27.200
So we had the key
as something like,

00:22:27.200 --> 00:22:28.580
finance tag or application.

00:22:28.580 --> 00:22:30.590
And then the actual
value of that would

00:22:30.590 --> 00:22:33.980
be the actual instance name.

00:22:33.980 --> 00:22:36.650
So first caveat.

00:22:36.650 --> 00:22:40.400
Second caveat is-- so I'm
forgetting some of my notes

00:22:40.400 --> 00:22:41.810
here.

00:22:41.810 --> 00:22:43.710
Ahem.

00:22:43.710 --> 00:22:44.957
Let's move on.

00:22:44.957 --> 00:22:47.040
So this is an example of
the Data Studio dashboard

00:22:47.040 --> 00:22:48.795
that we had initially created.

00:22:48.795 --> 00:22:50.670
As you can see, this is
the price application

00:22:50.670 --> 00:22:52.170
that Ron mentioned
a little bit ago.

00:22:52.170 --> 00:22:53.790
And you can see the
components here--

00:22:53.790 --> 00:22:56.503
api, redis, solr, cassandra.

00:22:56.503 --> 00:22:58.920
The actual design is not what
I really want to talk about.

00:22:58.920 --> 00:23:01.660
It's not the meat of
this presentation.

00:23:01.660 --> 00:23:03.960
How we filter, how we
group everything together

00:23:03.960 --> 00:23:06.370
is what is important here.

00:23:06.370 --> 00:23:09.360
So we use a feature that is
within Data Studio itself,

00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:11.300
called filtering.

00:23:11.300 --> 00:23:13.140
And since it is not
coming from BigQuery,

00:23:13.140 --> 00:23:15.510
all of these filters that
we apply are all manual.

00:23:15.510 --> 00:23:17.610
So for each
application team that's

00:23:17.610 --> 00:23:20.100
deploying into Google Cloud,
we created a new dashboard

00:23:20.100 --> 00:23:20.850
for them.

00:23:20.850 --> 00:23:22.810
We created a new
filter for them.

00:23:22.810 --> 00:23:25.650
And so as you can imagine,
as our teams grow,

00:23:25.650 --> 00:23:27.150
as the applications
grow in Google,

00:23:27.150 --> 00:23:29.180
it gets really
cumbersome because,

00:23:29.180 --> 00:23:31.755
for the initial migration, the
40 or so applications that we

00:23:31.755 --> 00:23:33.630
had, it was more of like
a mental list in Ron

00:23:33.630 --> 00:23:34.740
and I's mind.

00:23:34.740 --> 00:23:36.660
We know the Price
Application team.

00:23:36.660 --> 00:23:38.710
We know who owns it
at a broader level.

00:23:38.710 --> 00:23:41.142
But when you start having
teams that change ownership,

00:23:41.142 --> 00:23:42.600
or they start
changing code names--

00:23:42.600 --> 00:23:44.340
and for reference,
everybody loves

00:23:44.340 --> 00:23:46.770
to put the word "red" in every
of the code names we have.

00:23:46.770 --> 00:23:48.957
So you've got 20 red somewhere.

00:23:48.957 --> 00:23:50.040
It gets really cumbersome.

00:23:50.040 --> 00:23:51.030
It gets really manual.

00:23:51.030 --> 00:23:54.700
It's something that can't
scale with all of our apps.

00:23:54.700 --> 00:23:55.843
Now, another problem.

00:23:55.843 --> 00:23:57.510
This is just for teams
that are directly

00:23:57.510 --> 00:23:59.173
deploying on virtual machines.

00:23:59.173 --> 00:24:01.590
Ron mentioned that we brought
our own Kubernetes clusters.

00:24:01.590 --> 00:24:03.810
And roughly, about half
of the compute that we use

00:24:03.810 --> 00:24:05.690
are actually
Kubernetes clusters.

00:24:05.690 --> 00:24:07.620
And those Kubernetes
clusters are essentially

00:24:07.620 --> 00:24:10.435
virtual machines in
compute engine, right?

00:24:10.435 --> 00:24:12.310
So that label that's
getting applied to them,

00:24:12.310 --> 00:24:14.310
they're all getting tagged
as Kubernetes Master,

00:24:14.310 --> 00:24:15.657
or Kubernetes Node.

00:24:15.657 --> 00:24:18.240
And when we need to actually go
down and break down that usage

00:24:18.240 --> 00:24:20.100
and see who's using
what, it's something

00:24:20.100 --> 00:24:22.240
that we can't do
with Data Studio.

00:24:22.240 --> 00:24:25.032
So that leads us to a
different dashboard.

00:24:25.032 --> 00:24:26.490
Now, this is a tool
called Grafana,

00:24:26.490 --> 00:24:29.410
which I mentioned before as part
of Target's measurement stack.

00:24:29.410 --> 00:24:31.077
And so what we ended
up doing is writing

00:24:31.077 --> 00:24:34.220
an API that goes in and
grabs the total number of CPU

00:24:34.220 --> 00:24:37.200
and memory requests
per namespace

00:24:37.200 --> 00:24:39.150
and assigns a relative
cost, compared

00:24:39.150 --> 00:24:43.320
to the total number of CPU
memory requests per cluster.

00:24:43.320 --> 00:24:45.750
And we got the total cost of
the cluster from those labels

00:24:45.750 --> 00:24:48.600
that we were using, since it's
still getting tagged here.

00:24:48.600 --> 00:24:51.450
Now, this dashboard and the one
that I just had before roughly

00:24:51.450 --> 00:24:52.500
show the same thing--

00:24:52.500 --> 00:24:56.310
the breakdown per application
over a period of time,

00:24:56.310 --> 00:24:57.210
and per environment.

00:24:57.210 --> 00:25:00.900
We've got both our test
project and production project,

00:25:00.900 --> 00:25:02.800
as well.

00:25:02.800 --> 00:25:04.500
Now, what if you're
an application team

00:25:04.500 --> 00:25:06.450
and you're deploying
both containers

00:25:06.450 --> 00:25:07.663
and virtual machines?

00:25:07.663 --> 00:25:09.705
One of the questions that
I always got was, well,

00:25:09.705 --> 00:25:10.710
can you just tell me one number?

00:25:10.710 --> 00:25:13.085
Like, I don't want to have to
go to two different places.

00:25:13.085 --> 00:25:15.630
Like, I know it's
super simple, right?

00:25:15.630 --> 00:25:19.710
And so we did the most high
tech thing we could think of,

00:25:19.710 --> 00:25:22.470
and we sent all of this data
into an Excel spreadsheet,

00:25:22.470 --> 00:25:25.652
on a manual basis,
on a weekly basis.

00:25:25.652 --> 00:25:28.110
And just so you know, I still
get asked for the spreadsheet

00:25:28.110 --> 00:25:29.360
today.

00:25:29.360 --> 00:25:31.773
You know, we did
this two years ago.

00:25:31.773 --> 00:25:33.690
So just to recap-- all
of our virtual machines

00:25:33.690 --> 00:25:36.148
that were getting deployed were
getting tagged with labels.

00:25:36.148 --> 00:25:37.920
And that was going
into Data Studio.

00:25:37.920 --> 00:25:39.338
All of our Kubernetes
deployments,

00:25:39.338 --> 00:25:41.880
our container deployments, were
getting tagged using that API

00:25:41.880 --> 00:25:43.415
and breaking down by namespace.

00:25:43.415 --> 00:25:45.540
And that was going into
another visualization tool,

00:25:45.540 --> 00:25:46.475
called Grafana.

00:25:46.475 --> 00:25:47.850
And since we split
the two up, we

00:25:47.850 --> 00:25:50.970
had to marry them back together
in this Excel spreadsheet.

00:25:50.970 --> 00:25:52.422
That alone is a
number of problems

00:25:52.422 --> 00:25:53.880
that I don't even
want to get into.

00:25:53.880 --> 00:25:56.682
And I'm sure you can
guess what those are.

00:25:56.682 --> 00:25:58.890
But at this point, we're
just tagging compute, right?

00:25:58.890 --> 00:26:01.057
I mean, granted, compute
is a huge percentage of our

00:26:01.057 --> 00:26:03.120
build-- like 80% or
90% at this point.

00:26:03.120 --> 00:26:05.520
But we're largely
ignoring every other cost.

00:26:05.520 --> 00:26:07.620
I mean, we had a handful
of cloud storage buckets.

00:26:07.620 --> 00:26:10.892
We had a handful of
cloud SQL instances.

00:26:10.892 --> 00:26:12.600
And for both of those
cases, those things

00:26:12.600 --> 00:26:14.580
can be labeled and tagged.

00:26:14.580 --> 00:26:16.140
And we were just ignoring them.

00:26:16.140 --> 00:26:19.017
We labeled it as an
overhead cost and moved on.

00:26:19.017 --> 00:26:20.850
But then we had all
these other things, too,

00:26:20.850 --> 00:26:23.308
like the Stackdriver monitoring
and then certain networking

00:26:23.308 --> 00:26:26.830
costs that can't be tied
back to an instance.

00:26:26.830 --> 00:26:29.110
And then one last problem
is that this is just

00:26:29.110 --> 00:26:30.900
a test project and a
production project.

00:26:30.900 --> 00:26:32.692
Granted, they were the
two largest projects

00:26:32.692 --> 00:26:34.770
within our entire org,
but we had a handful

00:26:34.770 --> 00:26:36.720
of other projects that
were proof of concepts

00:26:36.720 --> 00:26:39.630
or for testing reasons
that we're also ignoring.

00:26:39.630 --> 00:26:42.010
So this initial
migration into the Google

00:26:42.010 --> 00:26:44.010
Cloud, again, like I said,
it was about 40 or so

00:26:44.010 --> 00:26:45.660
applications.

00:26:45.660 --> 00:26:48.338
We were tagging most
of what we could.

00:26:48.338 --> 00:26:50.130
But let's think back
to that first question

00:26:50.130 --> 00:26:51.600
that I asked everybody, right?

00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:54.300
Does this provide information
that teams can use in order

00:26:54.300 --> 00:26:55.380
to make better decisions?

00:26:55.380 --> 00:26:56.670
Can I look at one
of these dashboards

00:26:56.670 --> 00:26:59.128
and say, I know that's a good
number, or I know that's bad,

00:26:59.128 --> 00:27:01.833
or I know that's too high,
or I know where my waste is?

00:27:01.833 --> 00:27:03.000
And the answer is still, no.

00:27:05.713 --> 00:27:06.630
RON CUIRLE: All right.

00:27:06.630 --> 00:27:08.088
I want to mention
briefly something

00:27:08.088 --> 00:27:09.810
that Rachel
mentioned, and that's

00:27:09.810 --> 00:27:12.750
all of the
un-allocable costs that

00:27:12.750 --> 00:27:15.990
came out of our first pass.

00:27:15.990 --> 00:27:17.850
She said that they
have a tendency

00:27:17.850 --> 00:27:22.830
to just kind of be dismissed,
or assigned to the platform team

00:27:22.830 --> 00:27:25.620
incorrectly, namely
our teams, such

00:27:25.620 --> 00:27:30.030
that it looks like we are
10% of our entire total bill.

00:27:30.030 --> 00:27:33.780
When in fact, we're
probably 1% or less.

00:27:33.780 --> 00:27:38.280
We just have everything
that isn't allocable.

00:27:38.280 --> 00:27:38.820
All right.

00:27:38.820 --> 00:27:41.730
Anyway, thus far in
our journey, Target

00:27:41.730 --> 00:27:46.470
had chosen to run only
non-commerce workloads related

00:27:46.470 --> 00:27:48.300
to target.com at Google.

00:27:48.300 --> 00:27:51.210
Some of that was in support
of mobile, some not.

00:27:51.210 --> 00:27:54.510
These workloads are all about
the public information that's

00:27:54.510 --> 00:27:56.040
available on our website.

00:27:56.040 --> 00:27:58.290
You go to the website, you
browse, you see a price,

00:27:58.290 --> 00:28:02.430
you see an item, those are API
endpoints that is not secret.

00:28:02.430 --> 00:28:06.210
There's not a ton of security
that we need to put out there

00:28:06.210 --> 00:28:08.950
around those kinds of controls.

00:28:08.950 --> 00:28:09.870
But that security?

00:28:09.870 --> 00:28:12.150
Relatively simple,
in comparison to what

00:28:12.150 --> 00:28:13.660
we were about to undertake.

00:28:13.660 --> 00:28:18.240
So in July of 2018, we started
to migrate our commerce

00:28:18.240 --> 00:28:20.190
workloads to Google Cloud.

00:28:20.190 --> 00:28:24.780
And that, of course, came
with one huge, new big rock--

00:28:24.780 --> 00:28:27.840
regulatory compliance.

00:28:27.840 --> 00:28:28.380
Anybody?

00:28:28.380 --> 00:28:32.460
OK, we've got-- yes, this
is a problem, definitely

00:28:32.460 --> 00:28:33.090
from security.

00:28:33.090 --> 00:28:38.563
So we knew we needed
to look at organizing

00:28:38.563 --> 00:28:39.980
our resources a
little bit better,

00:28:39.980 --> 00:28:45.860
to be able to manage that
regulatory compliance easier.

00:28:45.860 --> 00:28:48.230
And we had a chance
to apply at least some

00:28:48.230 --> 00:28:52.853
of what we had done to fix some
of those pesky little tripping

00:28:52.853 --> 00:28:54.770
stones that we had tripped
on-- project naming

00:28:54.770 --> 00:28:57.120
conventions, application
code names, and more.

00:28:57.120 --> 00:28:58.310
So let's take a look.

00:28:58.310 --> 00:28:59.300
Get out your phones.

00:28:59.300 --> 00:29:02.270
This is the one that you want
to take a picture of, right?

00:29:02.270 --> 00:29:05.400
And the second or third
to last slide as well.

00:29:05.400 --> 00:29:11.870
This is from our colleagues that
are presenting Networking 200,

00:29:11.870 --> 00:29:13.670
A Year In GCP Networking.

00:29:13.670 --> 00:29:16.800
Unfortunately, that talk
is happening right now--

00:29:16.800 --> 00:29:19.210
please don't get up and leave--

00:29:19.210 --> 00:29:20.430
in another room.

00:29:20.430 --> 00:29:24.560
But similar to the talk
that is happening right now,

00:29:24.560 --> 00:29:31.160
the same colleagues are giving
a talk called Architecture 208,

00:29:31.160 --> 00:29:36.350
70 Days PCI Compliance to
Production With a Hybrid Cloud.

00:29:36.350 --> 00:29:38.930
That's right, 70 business
days using this design

00:29:38.930 --> 00:29:41.253
to get all of our commerce
workloads out there.

00:29:41.253 --> 00:29:43.670
And one of our presenters is
sitting here in the front row

00:29:43.670 --> 00:29:47.720
with a big, wide smile,
going tee, hee, hee.

00:29:47.720 --> 00:29:48.220
All right.

00:29:48.220 --> 00:29:49.970
Let's talk about some
of the lessons here.

00:29:49.970 --> 00:29:51.770
For our purposes
today, I want to be

00:29:51.770 --> 00:29:55.040
able to call out some huge
benefits of this design that

00:29:55.040 --> 00:29:59.000
translate into better
tracking, better billing costs.

00:29:59.000 --> 00:30:02.700
First, we learned
that leveraging

00:30:02.700 --> 00:30:08.250
every available capability of
a shared VPC is a no-brainer.

00:30:08.250 --> 00:30:12.420
One of those capabilities
is putting resources

00:30:12.420 --> 00:30:16.020
in one project within
an organization,

00:30:16.020 --> 00:30:18.480
but allowing
communication to happen

00:30:18.480 --> 00:30:23.860
between the projects using a
shared subnet, for example.

00:30:23.860 --> 00:30:25.800
Define all the
subnets in one area,

00:30:25.800 --> 00:30:28.320
but define the actual
resources in each

00:30:28.320 --> 00:30:31.035
of the compute projects,
or in their own project.

00:30:31.035 --> 00:30:33.330
I should refer to the notes.

00:30:33.330 --> 00:30:35.970
Specifically, I say the subnets
there and the compute engine

00:30:35.970 --> 00:30:38.320
resources in their own projects.

00:30:38.320 --> 00:30:39.540
So what does this do?

00:30:39.540 --> 00:30:42.090
Now, when a team spins
up a compute resource,

00:30:42.090 --> 00:30:45.210
it's in a project assigned to
that team and only that team.

00:30:45.210 --> 00:30:46.780
Yay.

00:30:46.780 --> 00:30:50.400
But Rachel's going to talk
in a little bit about how

00:30:50.400 --> 00:30:53.010
that's still a problem.

00:30:53.010 --> 00:30:56.280
Number two, platform components
that were previously getting

00:30:56.280 --> 00:30:58.830
all of the un-allocable costs.

00:30:58.830 --> 00:31:00.570
Look, we've got our
own project now,

00:31:00.570 --> 00:31:02.778
and we get treated just like
every application that's

00:31:02.778 --> 00:31:03.450
out there.

00:31:03.450 --> 00:31:04.530
So that's good.

00:31:04.530 --> 00:31:07.452
And we get project level
IAM all of a sudden

00:31:07.452 --> 00:31:08.910
that we didn't have
before, that we

00:31:08.910 --> 00:31:12.690
had to build our own security
around, and was just nasty.

00:31:12.690 --> 00:31:17.970
Third, app teams can consume
now Google-specific services.

00:31:17.970 --> 00:31:19.770
And their usage gets
attached directly

00:31:19.770 --> 00:31:22.625
to that project that's
allocable to that team.

00:31:22.625 --> 00:31:24.750
It doesn't matter that
they're using something that

00:31:24.750 --> 00:31:27.410
can't be tagged anymore, right?

00:31:27.410 --> 00:31:28.390
Maybe.

00:31:28.390 --> 00:31:30.140
Everything in the
project belongs to them,

00:31:30.140 --> 00:31:34.010
but maybe it still matters
that we can't tag it.

00:31:34.010 --> 00:31:36.326
Four-- quota management.

00:31:36.326 --> 00:31:38.030
Oh, wow.

00:31:38.030 --> 00:31:40.700
I'm sure our partners at
Google were very, very

00:31:40.700 --> 00:31:43.370
happy to stop getting
our phone calls about,

00:31:43.370 --> 00:31:47.240
we need another 2,000 instances
raised in our production

00:31:47.240 --> 00:31:49.700
environment, or give
us another 10,000 this,

00:31:49.700 --> 00:31:51.710
or we need more load balancers.

00:31:51.710 --> 00:31:52.643
Who that knows what?

00:31:52.643 --> 00:31:54.560
But regardless, there
is still a problem here.

00:31:54.560 --> 00:31:57.560
Every application
team could be putting

00:31:57.560 --> 00:32:03.103
more than just their application
into a particular project.

00:32:03.103 --> 00:32:04.520
Rachel's going to
talk about that.

00:32:04.520 --> 00:32:09.150
We need some sort of
better identification.

00:32:09.150 --> 00:32:12.980
So back to our second
highlight, or second takeaway,

00:32:12.980 --> 00:32:14.810
from the top of the slide deck--

00:32:14.810 --> 00:32:15.920
lowest common denominator.

00:32:15.920 --> 00:32:21.570
So introducing the configuration
item ID, yay, ITIL users.

00:32:21.570 --> 00:32:23.610
Everybody who follows
ITIL, life is great.

00:32:23.610 --> 00:32:25.400
It's an eight-digit
unique number

00:32:25.400 --> 00:32:26.995
across all of Target's
business units.

00:32:26.995 --> 00:32:28.370
And you remember
at the beginning

00:32:28.370 --> 00:32:31.280
when we were talking
about TAP, the goal of tap

00:32:31.280 --> 00:32:36.710
is to deploy things the
Target way across our fleet.

00:32:36.710 --> 00:32:39.020
It is not the store's
way, not the Google Cloud

00:32:39.020 --> 00:32:40.100
way, the Target way.

00:32:40.100 --> 00:32:41.840
CIs?

00:32:41.840 --> 00:32:43.490
A target identifier.

00:32:43.490 --> 00:32:44.570
Lovely.

00:32:44.570 --> 00:32:48.030
They are our lowest
common denominator.

00:32:48.030 --> 00:32:50.990
And what if we could apply
them to everything at Google?

00:32:50.990 --> 00:32:51.860
Great idea, right?

00:32:51.860 --> 00:32:54.942
Except it's not a new idea.

00:32:54.942 --> 00:32:56.900
A lot of you in the room
are going, yeah, yeah.

00:32:56.900 --> 00:32:57.810
We do this already.

00:32:57.810 --> 00:32:58.370
This is fine.

00:32:58.370 --> 00:33:00.390
No big deal.

00:33:00.390 --> 00:33:05.280
But our additional challenge
is if an application team

00:33:05.280 --> 00:33:09.630
has no financial responsibility
to tag their stuff,

00:33:09.630 --> 00:33:13.090
or voluntarily tag
their resources,

00:33:13.090 --> 00:33:16.290
how do we leverage
tech to do it for us?

00:33:16.290 --> 00:33:20.280
How do we enforce, or at least
manage, the data consistency?

00:33:20.280 --> 00:33:22.680
How can we ensure
complete coverage of all

00:33:22.680 --> 00:33:25.320
of those resources at Google?

00:33:25.320 --> 00:33:29.890
Hello Cloud Functions. ,
Again maybe not a new idea.

00:33:29.890 --> 00:33:32.110
We ask our teams to
label their resources.

00:33:32.110 --> 00:33:36.052
But if they mess it up, if
they screw it up, that's OK.

00:33:36.052 --> 00:33:38.010
We have Cloud Functions
in place for the launch

00:33:38.010 --> 00:33:39.240
of every compute resource.

00:33:39.240 --> 00:33:41.880
That allows for the
sanitation and standardization

00:33:41.880 --> 00:33:44.310
of the configuration item data.

00:33:44.310 --> 00:33:47.190
If teams don't supply
a CI at all, that's OK.

00:33:47.190 --> 00:33:48.730
What do we do?

00:33:48.730 --> 00:33:50.340
Well, if we really
care to, we could

00:33:50.340 --> 00:33:53.520
have that Cloud Function prevent
that instance from starting up,

00:33:53.520 --> 00:33:55.230
and just say, mm!

00:33:55.230 --> 00:33:58.060
Our security teams really
like this idea, by the way.

00:33:58.060 --> 00:34:03.180
But since it's really about
making it easy for app teams

00:34:03.180 --> 00:34:05.820
to consume, that seemed
a little extreme.

00:34:05.820 --> 00:34:08.620
So how do we track this?

00:34:08.620 --> 00:34:11.190
Hello, Forseti.

00:34:11.190 --> 00:34:12.719
It's pitched as a security tool.

00:34:12.719 --> 00:34:15.940
But at its base, if you're
not familiar with it,

00:34:15.940 --> 00:34:19.260
it's just about
finding deviations

00:34:19.260 --> 00:34:22.320
from a desired standard.

00:34:22.320 --> 00:34:25.679
We run reports to identify
things that can have labels,

00:34:25.679 --> 00:34:29.820
but don't, things that have
a misconstrued label, that

00:34:29.820 --> 00:34:32.639
indicate that we need
to have better logic

00:34:32.639 --> 00:34:35.010
inside of our functions.

00:34:35.010 --> 00:34:37.830
Notification is manual for
now, but there's nothing

00:34:37.830 --> 00:34:39.389
stopping us from
spending some time

00:34:39.389 --> 00:34:44.429
and taking our best guess at who
actually owns that application

00:34:44.429 --> 00:34:47.980
and automating a report to
be sent back out to them.

00:34:47.980 --> 00:34:50.159
We also have some
pretty crazy ideas

00:34:50.159 --> 00:34:53.100
about making that backend
more robust and automated that

00:34:53.100 --> 00:34:56.400
involve Pub/Sub, that involve
Stackdriver monitoring, that

00:34:56.400 --> 00:34:59.760
involve cloud tasks and
even machine learning

00:34:59.760 --> 00:35:03.450
to help us be even smarter
about these decisions.

00:35:03.450 --> 00:35:06.270
While you're here this
week, keep a close eye

00:35:06.270 --> 00:35:11.180
on alphas, betas,
GAs being announced.

00:35:11.180 --> 00:35:13.290
I've been scrubbed from
talking about the programs

00:35:13.290 --> 00:35:17.610
that we use, but there are
exciting things on the horizon

00:35:17.610 --> 00:35:19.710
that will make some
of the challenges

00:35:19.710 --> 00:35:21.600
that we're talking
about and mentioned

00:35:21.600 --> 00:35:26.662
in the last few slides all
of a sudden very, very easy.

00:35:26.662 --> 00:35:27.620
RACHEL SHINN: Alrighty.

00:35:27.620 --> 00:35:29.240
So we've got these
configuration items

00:35:29.240 --> 00:35:32.150
that we're now applying to
everything in Google Cloud.

00:35:32.150 --> 00:35:33.817
Ron and I are
thrilled, because that

00:35:33.817 --> 00:35:35.900
means the end of all that
manual filtering that we

00:35:35.900 --> 00:35:37.300
had to do in Data Studio.

00:35:37.300 --> 00:35:40.220
And instead, you see a dashboard
like this where, at the top,

00:35:40.220 --> 00:35:43.730
you can dropdown and choose your
specific configuration item,

00:35:43.730 --> 00:35:45.710
or perhaps a cluster
of configuration items

00:35:45.710 --> 00:35:47.493
that apply to your application.

00:35:47.493 --> 00:35:49.910
And you can also dropdown and
select your specific project

00:35:49.910 --> 00:35:50.870
that you want to see.

00:35:50.870 --> 00:35:53.840
And you can see all of the
data that you could see before.

00:35:53.840 --> 00:35:55.340
In this case for
showing compute,

00:35:55.340 --> 00:35:57.290
it's also tagging cloud storage.

00:35:57.290 --> 00:35:58.430
It's taking cloud SQL.

00:35:58.430 --> 00:36:01.340
Everything that can be
tagged is getting tagged.

00:36:01.340 --> 00:36:02.990
Yay, right?

00:36:02.990 --> 00:36:05.700
No, if it's not what happened.

00:36:05.700 --> 00:36:07.880
So some teams took
our advice, and they

00:36:07.880 --> 00:36:09.380
started tagging
different components

00:36:09.380 --> 00:36:12.440
with each different CI
or configuration item.

00:36:12.440 --> 00:36:15.112
Other teams just kept on doing
what they were doing before.

00:36:15.112 --> 00:36:16.820
And they used the same
configuration item

00:36:16.820 --> 00:36:19.460
across all of the components
in their application.

00:36:19.460 --> 00:36:21.920
Other teams, who managed
multiple applications,

00:36:21.920 --> 00:36:26.105
used one configuration item
across multiple applications.

00:36:26.105 --> 00:36:27.230
So this didn't work, right?

00:36:27.230 --> 00:36:29.647
We wanted to be able to break
down our costs even further.

00:36:29.647 --> 00:36:32.210
We wanted to be able to
allocate where the spending was

00:36:32.210 --> 00:36:33.900
coming down, even further.

00:36:33.900 --> 00:36:36.908
But that didn't really happen.

00:36:36.908 --> 00:36:38.450
And again, we still
don't really have

00:36:38.450 --> 00:36:40.790
a way of providing
real time increases

00:36:40.790 --> 00:36:42.470
and decreases, as
teams make updates

00:36:42.470 --> 00:36:44.470
to their infrastructure,
which is something that

00:36:44.470 --> 00:36:46.303
has been on the back
of our minds, something

00:36:46.303 --> 00:36:49.910
we wanted to do, but we still
hadn't quite figured out.

00:36:49.910 --> 00:36:51.320
So we kept our old dashboards.

00:36:51.320 --> 00:36:53.780
We kept all of the
manual filtering,

00:36:53.780 --> 00:36:56.448
even after we kept growing
in our footprint at Google.

00:36:56.448 --> 00:36:58.490
I mean, we started with
those additional 40 apps,

00:36:58.490 --> 00:37:00.200
and we've grown beyond that now.

00:37:00.200 --> 00:37:02.065
And we still have
these manual filters.

00:37:02.065 --> 00:37:03.440
Although, I guess,
the nice thing

00:37:03.440 --> 00:37:04.550
is that once you
build them, you're

00:37:04.550 --> 00:37:06.290
done where you don't have
to go in and change them

00:37:06.290 --> 00:37:07.730
or anything like
that, hopefully,

00:37:07.730 --> 00:37:10.273
and assuming teams don't change
their names or ownerships.

00:37:10.273 --> 00:37:11.690
But if you look
at this dashboard,

00:37:11.690 --> 00:37:13.650
it's slightly different than the
one that I showed you before.

00:37:13.650 --> 00:37:15.942
And that's because we edited
our filters just slightly.

00:37:15.942 --> 00:37:18.762
So we showed beyond
application dash component.

00:37:18.762 --> 00:37:20.720
At this point, we're
showing different versions

00:37:20.720 --> 00:37:21.920
of components.

00:37:21.920 --> 00:37:24.080
You can see here,
for price, their API,

00:37:24.080 --> 00:37:25.927
they've got a version
1, and version 2.

00:37:25.927 --> 00:37:28.010
You can also see that
they're in multiple regions.

00:37:28.010 --> 00:37:29.845
So we've got breakdowns
by region, as well.

00:37:29.845 --> 00:37:31.970
And essentially, these are
the instance groups that

00:37:31.970 --> 00:37:32.870
are getting deployed, right?

00:37:32.870 --> 00:37:34.800
I mean, initially, we we're
grabbing the instance name.

00:37:34.800 --> 00:37:36.425
And that's where that
label comes from.

00:37:36.425 --> 00:37:39.050
But at this point, we're able
to group by instance group now.

00:37:39.050 --> 00:37:41.240
And most of our apps use
instance groups to deploy,

00:37:41.240 --> 00:37:43.620
rather than just deploying
a single instance at a time.

00:37:43.620 --> 00:37:45.787
RON CUIRLE: Can you talk
about why that's important,

00:37:45.787 --> 00:37:46.780
before you move on?

00:37:46.780 --> 00:37:47.530
RACHEL SHINN: Why?

00:37:47.530 --> 00:37:52.310
RON CUIRLE: All of this is if
a team wants more information

00:37:52.310 --> 00:37:53.450
to make better decisions.

00:37:53.450 --> 00:37:53.660
RACHEL SHINN: Yes.

00:37:53.660 --> 00:37:55.118
RON CUIRLE: How
are they ever going

00:37:55.118 --> 00:37:56.960
to know that version
2 of an application

00:37:56.960 --> 00:38:00.260
is performing better and using
less money than version 1,

00:38:00.260 --> 00:38:01.443
if we don't have this?

00:38:01.443 --> 00:38:03.860
RACHEL SHINN: And that's what
these dashboards did, right?

00:38:03.860 --> 00:38:06.610
So you can see that they've
got multiple versions deployed.

00:38:06.610 --> 00:38:07.970
And their versions might
be different, right?

00:38:07.970 --> 00:38:09.387
They make an update
to their code,

00:38:09.387 --> 00:38:11.570
they make a different
modification or something,

00:38:11.570 --> 00:38:13.042
and they redeploy.

00:38:13.042 --> 00:38:14.000
And we can monitor now.

00:38:14.000 --> 00:38:14.900
We can see the changes.

00:38:14.900 --> 00:38:16.733
We can see the disparencies
between the two,

00:38:16.733 --> 00:38:19.772
and they can decide
which one's better.

00:38:19.772 --> 00:38:21.230
So where does that
leave us, right?

00:38:21.230 --> 00:38:22.910
Let's think back to
that initial question.

00:38:22.910 --> 00:38:24.410
Does this provide
enough information

00:38:24.410 --> 00:38:27.240
that teams can use to make
those better decisions?

00:38:27.240 --> 00:38:28.240
The answer's still, no.

00:38:28.240 --> 00:38:30.130
Unfortunately, right?

00:38:30.130 --> 00:38:31.820
We're not providing
enough information,

00:38:31.820 --> 00:38:35.670
/ it seems like cost data just
isn't cutting it at this point.

00:38:35.670 --> 00:38:38.570
And so as we looked to expanding
these dashboards beyond Google

00:38:38.570 --> 00:38:40.782
Cloud, we wanted
to integrate all

00:38:40.782 --> 00:38:42.740
of our other platforms
as well, potentially all

00:38:42.740 --> 00:38:43.942
of our managed services.

00:38:43.942 --> 00:38:46.400
We really had to figure out
why the answer to that question

00:38:46.400 --> 00:38:47.360
kept being no.

00:38:47.360 --> 00:38:50.150
And that's where we come up with
different kinds of information,

00:38:50.150 --> 00:38:53.100
specifically capacity
and efficiency.

00:38:53.100 --> 00:38:55.118
So we're going
through, we're tagging

00:38:55.118 --> 00:38:56.660
all of this other
information, right?

00:38:56.660 --> 00:38:57.950
We're looking at capacity--

00:38:57.950 --> 00:39:00.907
the total number of VMs
that I've got deployed.

00:39:00.907 --> 00:39:02.240
And we're looking at efficiency.

00:39:02.240 --> 00:39:05.390
So of those 100 VMs that I
might have just deployed,

00:39:05.390 --> 00:39:06.450
what am I actually using?

00:39:06.450 --> 00:39:07.880
Am I at 5% utilization?

00:39:07.880 --> 00:39:09.350
Am I at 10%?

00:39:09.350 --> 00:39:13.530
And those numbers are what we
can start conversations with.

00:39:13.530 --> 00:39:16.950
And obviously, you're all
familiar-- like, for capacity,

00:39:16.950 --> 00:39:18.530
I closely relate it to cost too.

00:39:18.530 --> 00:39:20.428
Because I spin up 100 VMs.

00:39:20.428 --> 00:39:21.470
That's my total capacity.

00:39:21.470 --> 00:39:24.100
That's total allocated number
of resources that I've got.

00:39:24.100 --> 00:39:25.190
And I pay for that, right?

00:39:25.190 --> 00:39:26.720
I pay for what
I've spun up, and I

00:39:26.720 --> 00:39:29.040
don't pay for what I
actually use of that.

00:39:29.040 --> 00:39:30.498
So it's really
important when we're

00:39:30.498 --> 00:39:33.120
talking about managing costs
and eliminating operating costs

00:39:33.120 --> 00:39:34.400
and everything like
that, because that starts

00:39:34.400 --> 00:39:36.025
that conversation
with those app teams,

00:39:36.025 --> 00:39:38.480
so that we can make
them more, not reliable,

00:39:38.480 --> 00:39:41.095
but make them more cognizant
of what their spending is.

00:39:41.095 --> 00:39:42.470
And again, like
I said, we really

00:39:42.470 --> 00:39:44.000
wanted to expand
our cost dashboards

00:39:44.000 --> 00:39:45.170
across all of our platforms.

00:39:45.170 --> 00:39:47.810
And so now, by combining the
three kinds of information,

00:39:47.810 --> 00:39:48.700
we can.

00:39:48.700 --> 00:39:51.390
And we can also target our
managed services, as well.

00:39:51.390 --> 00:39:53.190
And so when I say
managed services,

00:39:53.190 --> 00:39:54.770
we've got a couple of different
infrastructure teams that

00:39:54.770 --> 00:39:57.020
provide services for
application teams to use,

00:39:57.020 --> 00:39:59.600
such as database tools,
or measurement tools.

00:39:59.600 --> 00:40:02.000
And historically,
every database cost

00:40:02.000 --> 00:40:03.920
was attributed back
to the database team,

00:40:03.920 --> 00:40:06.200
even though a bunch
of application teams

00:40:06.200 --> 00:40:07.360
were using those databases.

00:40:07.360 --> 00:40:08.960
And so now what we can
do with this information

00:40:08.960 --> 00:40:10.710
is actually tie it
back to the application

00:40:10.710 --> 00:40:12.900
team that's using it.

00:40:12.900 --> 00:40:15.488
So where does that
leave us going forward?

00:40:15.488 --> 00:40:17.780
This is a pretty new initiative
at Target and something

00:40:17.780 --> 00:40:19.030
that we're still building out.

00:40:19.030 --> 00:40:22.100
But essentially, what we want
to do is capture allocation,

00:40:22.100 --> 00:40:25.055
utilization, and cost data
all together-- all of it,

00:40:25.055 --> 00:40:26.108
it's a ton of data--

00:40:26.108 --> 00:40:27.650
and at the deepest
level that we can.

00:40:27.650 --> 00:40:29.067
So for some of our
platforms, it's

00:40:29.067 --> 00:40:31.467
all the way down to an
individual host or server.

00:40:31.467 --> 00:40:32.300
Others, not so much.

00:40:32.300 --> 00:40:33.880
It just really depends
on the platform.

00:40:33.880 --> 00:40:35.060
But we're going to
export that data.

00:40:35.060 --> 00:40:36.050
We're going to
start publishing it

00:40:36.050 --> 00:40:37.467
so that teams can
actually see it.

00:40:37.467 --> 00:40:39.980
But before, it was just for
infrastructure teams, right?

00:40:39.980 --> 00:40:41.630
I could go in and
see what my platform,

00:40:41.630 --> 00:40:42.650
like the utilization of it.

00:40:42.650 --> 00:40:44.180
But I didn't share that
with the application

00:40:44.180 --> 00:40:45.240
teams using my platform.

00:40:45.240 --> 00:40:48.663
And so that was really a
change of how we did things.

00:40:48.663 --> 00:40:51.080
So we're exporting all this
data, either via Kafka topics,

00:40:51.080 --> 00:40:51.708
or an API.

00:40:51.708 --> 00:40:53.750
We're still kind of
understanding and identifying

00:40:53.750 --> 00:40:55.622
what the best practices
for long term,

00:40:55.622 --> 00:40:57.080
especially as some
of our platforms

00:40:57.080 --> 00:40:58.200
are a little bit more modern.

00:40:58.200 --> 00:41:00.075
And then some of them
are a little bit legacy

00:41:00.075 --> 00:41:00.910
in our data centers.

00:41:00.910 --> 00:41:03.470
So we really just need to
figure out what the best way is.

00:41:03.470 --> 00:41:05.660
And then we're ingesting all
of that data in a single place.

00:41:05.660 --> 00:41:08.285
And it's all getting aggregated
on our big data platform, which

00:41:08.285 --> 00:41:10.440
does live in our data centers.

00:41:10.440 --> 00:41:12.470
We're working with
our data science teams

00:41:12.470 --> 00:41:14.930
to build a couple of views
that we're not sending all

00:41:14.930 --> 00:41:16.100
of that data somewhere else.

00:41:16.100 --> 00:41:18.500
We're just sending what we
need, and we're displaying it

00:41:18.500 --> 00:41:21.890
on a single dashboard that you
can export that data into a CSV

00:41:21.890 --> 00:41:24.095
if you need it to get
that fine level detail.

00:41:24.095 --> 00:41:26.720
But essentially, this dashboard,
if I were an application team,

00:41:26.720 --> 00:41:29.060
I can go and say, here's
my configuration item,

00:41:29.060 --> 00:41:30.535
or here's my application.

00:41:30.535 --> 00:41:32.330
And I can everywhere
that it's deployed.

00:41:32.330 --> 00:41:34.070
I can see the total
allocated resources

00:41:34.070 --> 00:41:35.570
that I've got for
that configuration

00:41:35.570 --> 00:41:37.730
item, or for my
component, for my app.

00:41:37.730 --> 00:41:40.940
I can see the actual CPU and
memory utilization of that.

00:41:40.940 --> 00:41:43.810
And then I can see, if we can
provide it, attributable cost.

00:41:43.810 --> 00:41:45.530
So if any of you
are familiar, it's

00:41:45.530 --> 00:41:47.810
really hard to determine
specific costs for apps that

00:41:47.810 --> 00:41:49.380
are deployed in data centers.

00:41:49.380 --> 00:41:50.420
It's really easy for
Google, because I

00:41:50.420 --> 00:41:51.378
get a bill every month.

00:41:51.378 --> 00:41:53.990
But for data centers,
it's not the case.

00:41:53.990 --> 00:41:58.610
So favorite question-- does
this provide information?

00:41:58.610 --> 00:42:01.070
Can a team go into
this dashboard,

00:42:01.070 --> 00:42:04.200
see information, and make a
decision based off of that?

00:42:04.200 --> 00:42:07.532
And finally, we think the
answer is, yes, we hope so.

00:42:07.532 --> 00:42:08.990
Again like I said,
we're still kind

00:42:08.990 --> 00:42:10.865
of building out this
whole dashboard and what

00:42:10.865 --> 00:42:11.512
it looks like.

00:42:11.512 --> 00:42:13.220
But we have been
sending it out to teams,

00:42:13.220 --> 00:42:16.110
and it's been providing a
lot of great results already.

00:42:16.110 --> 00:42:18.110
And it really helps us
start those conversations

00:42:18.110 --> 00:42:19.860
with teams that might
be over-subscribing,

00:42:19.860 --> 00:42:22.868
or they might be over-allocating
resources to themselves,

00:42:22.868 --> 00:42:23.910
and they're not using it.

00:42:23.910 --> 00:42:26.202
And so it's just a way for
us to be a little bit better

00:42:26.202 --> 00:42:29.340
in efficiency and capacity.

00:42:29.340 --> 00:42:30.237
So just to recap--

00:42:30.237 --> 00:42:31.820
we talked about how
we organize things

00:42:31.820 --> 00:42:32.870
and why it didn't work for us.

00:42:32.870 --> 00:42:34.910
And then we talked about how
we did it differently, right?

00:42:34.910 --> 00:42:37.035
And don't want you to be
afraid of changing the way

00:42:37.035 --> 00:42:39.675
that things were,
assuming you can.

00:42:39.675 --> 00:42:41.300
We talked about the
common denominator,

00:42:41.300 --> 00:42:43.067
that's configuration item.

00:42:43.067 --> 00:42:44.900
It didn't work for us
great in Google Cloud,

00:42:44.900 --> 00:42:47.130
but it's something that was
a standard across Target.

00:42:47.130 --> 00:42:48.800
And so if you were
to go and request

00:42:48.800 --> 00:42:50.800
new resources for something
in our data centers,

00:42:50.800 --> 00:42:53.270
like you are required to enter
in that configuration item.

00:42:53.270 --> 00:42:55.670
That is a standard practice
across all of our platforms

00:42:55.670 --> 00:42:56.712
now.

00:42:56.712 --> 00:42:57.920
And so teams know what it is.

00:42:57.920 --> 00:42:58.340
They know it.

00:42:58.340 --> 00:42:59.030
They are expecting it.

00:42:59.030 --> 00:43:00.440
They know they need to do it.

00:43:00.440 --> 00:43:02.303
And then the products--

00:43:02.303 --> 00:43:03.470
Forseti and Cloud Functions.

00:43:03.470 --> 00:43:04.530
They're not used for billing.

00:43:04.530 --> 00:43:05.510
They weren't
marketed for billing,

00:43:05.510 --> 00:43:07.093
but we're using them
for that anyways,

00:43:07.093 --> 00:43:09.663
because we're making it work
for what we needed it to do.

00:43:09.663 --> 00:43:11.330
RON CUIRLE: Ubiquitous
compute platforms

00:43:11.330 --> 00:43:12.913
require a ubiquitous
billing platform.

00:43:12.913 --> 00:43:13.663
RACHEL SHINN: Yes.

00:43:13.663 --> 00:43:14.490
Yes.

00:43:14.490 --> 00:43:18.050
Ron mentioned the Target
application, or TAP, right?

00:43:18.050 --> 00:43:19.610
The idea was that
regardless of where

00:43:19.610 --> 00:43:20.810
you're deploying
your application,

00:43:20.810 --> 00:43:21.740
you go to one spot.

00:43:21.740 --> 00:43:23.400
And everything you
need is right there.

00:43:23.400 --> 00:43:25.025
That's the same
approach that we wanted

00:43:25.025 --> 00:43:26.420
to take with billing too, right?

00:43:26.420 --> 00:43:28.550
Just you go to one
spot, everything

00:43:28.550 --> 00:43:29.650
is there that you need.

00:43:29.650 --> 00:43:30.870
You don't need anything else.

00:43:30.870 --> 00:43:33.573
And with that, we'd
just love to thank you.

00:43:33.573 --> 00:43:34.490
RON CUIRLE: Thank you.

00:43:34.490 --> 00:43:35.000
RACHEL SHINN: It's exciting.

00:43:35.000 --> 00:43:35.270
Yeah.

00:43:35.270 --> 00:43:35.970
Thanks.

00:43:35.970 --> 00:43:36.570
[APPLAUSE]

00:43:36.570 --> 00:43:39.020
[MUSIC PLAYING]

